/* Consensus over inter-satellite links that are not instantaneous: the delay at which agreement
   stops being reached, for the two protocols a formation can actually run.

   n agents, a fixed connected undirected graph with non-negative weights a_ij, degree matrix
   D = diag(sum_j a_ij), adjacency A, Laplacian L = D - A. Every link carries the same delay tau.

   PROTOCOL A -- the relative measurement is what arrives late, so both terms are delayed:

       x_i'(t) = sum_j a_ij [ x_j(t - tau) - x_i(t - tau) ]      i.e.  x' = -L x(t - tau)

   PROTOCOL B -- the agent knows its own state now and its neighbour's as of tau ago:

       x_i'(t) = sum_j a_ij [ x_j(t - tau) - x_i(t) ]            i.e.  x' = -D x(t) + A x(t - tau)

   Same graph, same links, same latency. This file derives the delay at which each one loses
   consensus, because the two answers are not the same and the difference is the point of the
   ticket. Protocol A is the one Olfati-Saber & Murray (IEEE TAC 49(9), 2004) bound; Protocol B is
   what the ticket's own u_i = -sum_j K_ij [x_i(t) - x_j(t - tau_ij)] writes down.

   L is symmetric positive semi-definite with eigenvalues 0 = lam_1 <= ... <= lam_n, and lam_2 > 0
   exactly when the graph is connected. The weights are control gains, so lam carries units of 1/s
   and tau_max comes out in seconds without further scaling. */

declare([d, lam, w, tau], real)$
assume(d > 0, lam > 0, w > 0, tau > 0)$

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Protocol A */

/* L is symmetric, so an orthogonal change of basis takes x' = -L x(t - tau) to n scalar equations
   y_k' = -lam_k y_k(t - tau), one per eigenvalue, with no coupling left. The whole system is
   therefore stable exactly when every one of them is, and the binding mode is lam_max. */

ceq_a : lam_ + lam*exp(-lam_*tau)$

sub_a : expand(demoivre(subst(lam_ = %i*w, ceq_a)))$
re_a  : ratsimp(realpart(sub_a))$
im_a  : ratsimp(imagpart(sub_a))$
print("A: real part = 0 :", re_a)$
print("A: imag part = 0 :", im_a)$

/* Eliminate tau the same way delay_margin.mac does: c = cos(w tau), s = sin(w tau), resultant
   against the Pythagorean identity. What survives is a polynomial in w alone. */
rec_a : subst([cos(w*tau) = c, sin(w*tau) = s], re_a)$
imc_a : subst([cos(w*tau) = c, sin(w*tau) = s], im_a)$
poly_a : factor(first(eliminate([rec_a, imc_a, c^2 + s^2 - 1], [c, s])))$
print("A: crossing polynomial in w :", poly_a)$

/* It is (w - lam)(w + lam) up to sign; with w > 0 and lam > 0 the only crossing is w = lam. */
print("A: poly / ((w - lam)(w + lam)) (must be a constant) :",
      ratsimp(poly_a / ((w - lam)*(w + lam))))$

w_a : lam$
/* Real part 0 forces cos(w tau) = 0 and the imaginary part then fixes the sign of the sine, so the
   first crossing is w tau = pi/2 rather than 3pi/2. */
tau_a : %pi/(2*lam)$
print("A: w_c =", w_a, "  tau_max =", tau_a)$

/* Substituted back into the *original transcendental* equation, not into a rearrangement of it.
   Both parts must vanish, at bigfloat precision, or the pair is a solution of something else. */
fpprec : 40$
resid_a : subst([lam_ = %i*w_a, tau = tau_a], ceq_a)$
print("A: residual of the transcendental equation :",
      bfloat(subst(lam = 1, rectform(resid_a))))$

/* The product tau_max * lam_max is pi/2 for every graph and every weighting -- the shape of the
   answer, as in delay_margin.mac. A formation buys margin only by lowering the gain that produces
   lam_max, and doing that slows consensus by the same factor. */
print("A: tau_max * lam (must be pi/2) :", ratsimp(tau_a*lam))$

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ Protocol B */

/* D and A do not commute unless the graph is regular, so the general case does not decouple. Take
   the regular case in closed form here -- every degree equal to d, so A = d I - L and the modes
   separate again -- and bound the general case below. */

ceq_b : lam_ + d - (d - lam)*exp(-lam_*tau)$

sub_b : expand(demoivre(subst(lam_ = %i*w, ceq_b)))$
re_b  : ratsimp(realpart(sub_b))$
im_b  : ratsimp(imagpart(sub_b))$
print("B: real part = 0 :", re_b)$
print("B: imag part = 0 :", im_b)$

rec_b : subst([cos(w*tau) = c, sin(w*tau) = s], re_b)$
imc_b : subst([cos(w*tau) = c, sin(w*tau) = s], im_b)$
poly_b : factor(first(eliminate([rec_b, imc_b, c^2 + s^2 - 1], [c, s])))$
print("B: crossing polynomial in w :", poly_b)$

/* The physical factor is w^2 + lam (2 d - lam) and the cofactor is the perfect square (lam - d)^2,
   which carries no crossing. Divided out and printed rather than read off, so the claim is checked.

   The one value where the cofactor vanishes, lam = d, is not a crossing either: there A's row and
   D's diagonal cancel, the characteristic equation collapses to lam_ + d = 0 with no delay term at
   all, and the mode is stable at every tau. The resultant vanishing identically there is the
   elimination degenerating, not a root reaching the axis. */
crossing_b : w^2 + lam*(2*d - lam)$
print("B: poly / (w^2 + lam (2 d - lam)) (must be the square (lam - d)^2) :",
      ratsimp(poly_b/crossing_b))$
print("B: the equation at lam = d (no delay term survives) :",
      ratsimp(subst(lam = d, ceq_b)))$

/* w^2 = -lam (2 d - lam). For any graph 0 <= lam <= 2 d_max, and for a d-regular graph
   0 <= lam <= 2d exactly, so lam (2 d - lam) >= 0 and w^2 <= 0: there is no real crossing
   frequency at any positive delay. */

/* The two endpoints of that interval are where the argument would be tight, so both are checked
   rather than waved at. lam = 0 is the consensus direction itself, which must have a root at the
   origin -- it is the conserved quantity, not an instability. lam = 2 d is the bipartite case. */
print("B: at lam = 0, the equation at lam_ = 0 (a root at the origin, as required) :",
      ratsimp(subst([lam = 0, lam_ = 0], ceq_b)))$
print("B: at lam = 2 d, w = 0, the real part (must be non-zero, so no crossing) :",
      ratsimp(subst([lam = 2*d, w = 0], re_b)))$

/* General (non-regular) graph. A crossing at lam_ = %i w needs (%i w I + D) v = exp(-%i w tau) A v
   for some v != 0, hence v = exp(-%i w tau) (%i w I + D)^-1 A v, hence 1 <= || (%i w I + D)^-1 A ||
   in any induced norm. The inverse is diagonal with entries 1/(%i w + d_i), so the row-sum
   (infinity) norm of the product is max_i (sum_j a_ij)/|%i w + d_i| = max_i d_i/sqrt(w^2 + d_i^2).
   That is strictly below 1 for every w != 0, so no crossing exists off the origin -- whatever the
   degrees, whatever the weights, whatever the delay. */
print("B: d/sqrt(w^2 + d^2) < 1 for w /= 0, as  1 - (d/sqrt(w^2+d^2))^2 =",
      ratsimp(1 - d^2/(w^2 + d^2)))$

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ a number to quote */

/* A six-satellite ring (cycle C_6) with a uniform link gain k: the Laplacian eigenvalues are
   2k(1 - cos(2 pi j / 6)), j = 0..5, so lam_max = 4k. Derived rather than looked up. */
cycle_eig(nn, j, k) := 2*k*(1 - cos(2*%pi*j/nn))$
print("C_6 Laplacian spectrum / k :",
      makelist(ratsimp(cycle_eig(6, j, 1)), j, 0, 5))$
lam_max_c6 : ratsimp(apply(max, makelist(cycle_eig(6, j, 1), j, 0, 5)))$
print("C_6 lam_max / k :", lam_max_c6)$
print("C_6 tau_max at k = 0.01 /s :",
      bfloat(subst(lam = lam_max_c6*0.01, tau_a)), "s")$
